{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "def car_race_collision(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of head-on collisions between n left-to-right cars\n    and n right-to-left cars on an infinitely long straight road. Each car\n    moving in one direction will collide with every car moving in the opposite\n    direction, as all cars move at the same speed and pass through each other.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The number of cars moving in each direction. This must be a\n                 non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The total number of collisions, calculated as n * n.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If `n` is not an integer type or if `n` is a boolean.\n                   Boolean inputs are explicitly rejected even though `bool`\n                   is a subclass of `int`.\n        ValueError: If `n` is an integer but is negative.\n\n    Complexity:\n        Time: O(1) - The calculation involves a single multiplication and\n                      constant-time input validation.\n        Space: O(1) - No data structures are created that scale with `n`.\n\n    Notes:\n        This function supports arbitrarily large non-negative integer values for `n`,\n        limited only by the Python runtime's ability to handle large integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or isinstance(n, bool):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int and not a boolean\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be non-negative\")\n\n    return n * n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/42", "solution": "def map_increment(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Return a new list with each element incremented by 1.\"\"\"\n    return [elem + 1 for elem in l]\ndef incr_list(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return list with elements incremented by 1.\n    >>> incr_list([1, 2, 3])\n    [2, 3, 4]\n    >>> incr_list([5, 3, 5, 2, 3, 3, 9, 0, 123])\n    [6, 4, 6, 3, 4, 4, 10, 1, 124]\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_is_list(l)\n    validate_elements_are_integers(l)\n    return map_increment(l)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "import collections\nfrom typing import List\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(l, list) or len(l) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    for x in l:\n        if not isinstance(x, int):\n            return False\n\n    # A more efficient approach for checking pairs is using a hash set.\n    # We iterate through the list. For each number `num`, we check if its complement `-num`\n    # is already in our set of seen numbers.\n    # This automatically handles distinctness by index since we only add numbers to\n    # the set after checking for their complements.\n    # If the list contains [0, 0], this approach correctly finds a pair.\n    \n    seen_numbers = set()\n    for num in l:\n        complement = -num\n        if complement in seen_numbers:\n            return True\n        seen_numbers.add(num)\n        \n    return False"}
